


The black path connecting them did not match any marked route.




Maren filmed the two maps from directly above. Her hands shook enough that she braced her wrists against the wooden post.




“The article map is a composite,” she said. “It uses the shelter location, a river bend from farther north, the quarry edge, settlement remnants, and the crime-associated area as though they occupy one trail loop. They do not. The scale does not match the official map, and the connecting path is not part of the preserve trail system.”




Jonah did not look at the camera. He watched the woods beyond the staff path.




Maren continued. “The shelter is not the building described in the article. The reported grave has no identified location on the official map. The historical premise we used to plan this episode is wrong.”




A frog called from the low ground.




Jonah folded the route sheet once. “The map is wrong. The call is still on three recorders, and the impact happened while we were both outside.”




“We still have the call and the impact,” Maren said. “We don’t have the cabin, the grave, or the crime story.”




He pushed the folded sheet into his pocket. “You keep treating the correction like an ending.”




Her eyes went to the loose tape at his wrist.




He looked down at his bandaged hand. The tape near his wrist had lifted again. He pressed it flat and failed to make it hold.




Maren took fresh medical tape from the camera bag. He let her wrap the loose end without speaking. His skin felt cold, though they had been standing still under the trees.




When she finished, he flexed his fingers once.




“Then separate us,” he said.




“What does that mean?”




He pointed toward the blue trail. “You go back toward the shelter. I take the river loop to the next marker.”




“No.”




“We stay on marked trails. We keep radio contact. Ten minutes.”




“The agreement says no solo sessions.”




“The agreement was built around a place that isn’t what we thought it was.”




“That rule was built around you disappearing.”




He went still. Frogs called below the junction. The generator reached them only when the chorus paused.




“If we stay together, we hear the same sounds,” he said. “We can’t compare what happens near the shelter with what happens on my route.”




He raised the handheld recorder between them. “You take the shelter side. I take the river loop. If something happens on mine and not yours, then maybe it isn’t the shelter.”




Maren looked toward the two permitted routes. Correcting the map had only shifted Jonah’s theory. Now he wanted to separate himself from the shelter and compare what each of them heard.




Jonah looked down each marked route once more.




“Separate recordings,” he said. “Fifteen minutes.”




Scene 6 — The Split




At eleven thirty-six, Maren stood again at the northern junction with a fifteen-minute timer ready.




They had spent more than three hours returning to the shelter, making the ten o’clock check-in, shutting down the generator, packing the emitter, and recording two more listening sessions under cleaner conditions. Neither session had produced the cry, another impact, or any sound Maren could separate from the river, insects, branches, road traffic, and their own movement. Jonah had not repeated his proposal during the work. He had helped coil cables, replaced batteries, and logged the negative intervals in block letters with his left hand.




When they reached the junction again, he took out the handheld recorder.




Maren should have turned them toward the gate. Instead she placed the official map against the trail post and drew two short lines with the grease pencil. Jonah would take the white-marked river loop to its next numbered post. She would take the blue route toward the shelter and stop at the first marker. The two positions lay on separate arms of the junction but remained within the permitted trail system.




“Eleven fifty-one,” she said. “We start back whether anything happens or not. Junction by eleven fifty-four. Midnight check-in from here.”




Jonah looked at the marks. “Fifteen minutes.”




“Fifteen to turn back.”




He clipped the radio to his jacket. His phone went into the outer pocket beneath it, screen facing inward. The bandage on his right hand remained secure where she had retaped it.




Maren continued. “Radio at the first marker, then every two minutes. State your marker number before anything else. Light stays on. You do not leave the white route. You do not follow a sound. You do not go toward the staff path.”




He pressed the transmit button. Her radio clicked against her shoulder.




“White route, marker, light, contact,” he said. “I heard you.”




“No emitter. No headphones. Recorder and radio only.”




He held up the handheld recorder.




Maren turned the camera on herself long enough to state the time, location, route division, reunion time, and the rule they had already broken by separating. Then she filmed Jonah repeating his route and conditions. She did not ask him to explain what he expected to prove. His proposal was already on the earlier recording. Arguing again would not undo the separation she had just authorized.




She set both radios to the same volume and tested them from opposite sides of the post.




“Blue junction,” she said.




“White junction.”




His voice came through with a faint static edge but remained clear.




Maren started the timer. “Eleven thirty-six.”




Jonah raised two fingers and walked down the white route. She watched until his headlamp passed behind the first stand of brush, reappeared lower on the trail, and disappeared around the river bend.




She waited ten seconds before taking the blue route.




The path climbed away from the low ground. Her camera light covered the packed soil, exposed roots, and blue triangles fixed to the trunks. The shelter generator was off, and without its pulse the distance between the junction and the clearing seemed greater than it had earlier. Water remained audible behind her. Crickets filled the dry slope ahead. A frog called once from the low ground and stopped.




At the first blue marker, Maren filmed the triangle, the number beneath it, and the path in both directions. She set the camera on the tripod at the inside edge of the trail, where the frame included the marker, her position, and roughly thirty feet of the route behind her. Beyond that distance, the beam ended against leaves and thin trunks.




She placed the audio recorder on the post and held the route sheet beneath the camera light.




“Blue marker one,” she said into the radio.




A burst of static preceded Jonah’s reply. “White marker two.”




His breathing sounded fast, but the transmission was too short for her to know whether the walk explained it.




“Confirm light and route.”




“Light on. On white.”




Maren checked the time. Eleven thirty-nine.
